From a1c7e86771d122db22db9126795fb22d8708523d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Glib Shpychka <23005347+gshpychka@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:25:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Support the kitty clipboard protocol (OSC 5522) with paste
 events

Panes can turn on paste events (mode 5522) and read and write the
clipboard with OSC 5522, as the protocol's section on multiplexers
describes:
https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/clipboard/#support-for-terminal-multiplexers

tmux detects support with a DECRQM query for mode 5522 (feature
"osc5522", capabilities Enpe and Dspe). It sets the mode on the client
terminal while the active pane has it and no prompt or overlay is open.
Paste events carry no id and go to the active pane.

A pane's OSC 5522 read or write is a request to the most recently used
client, sent under an id of tmux's own. Replies with that id go back to
the pane with the pane's id restored. A request stays open until DONE or
an error status and times out after 30 seconds without a packet. It
doesn't hold back the pane's other replies. A client has one request at a
time, so another pane gets EBUSY, and a pane's new request replaces its
unfinished one.

The clipboard options apply as they do for OSC 52. A write needs
set-clipboard on, and its text/plain data creates a paste buffer. Without
a client that supports the protocol, tmux completes the write itself. A
read follows get-clipboard: off refuses it with EPERM, buffer answers with
the newest paste buffer as text/plain, and request and both relay it;
both also creates a paste buffer from its text/plain data. A read with the
one-time password of a paste event the pane received is always relayed.
A relayed read gets ENOSYS when the client doesn't support the protocol.
